PR #393  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Linker relaxation incorrectly substitutes compressed/non-compressed instruction sequences when EF_RISCV_RVC cannot encode mixed rvc/norvc regions within one object, producing silently corrupted machine code.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/elf.h:89-89  (none — UAPI header)
      Defines R_RISCV_RELAX=51 as the ELF relocation type constant. This is the canonical ABI definition mirrored from the psABI spec. The kernel doesn't interpret or apply this relocation itself, only passes it through. No violation here — the constant definition is correct.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/module.c:286-290  apply_r_riscv_relax_rela
      Returns 0 (no-op) because the linker already applied the relaxation. This is correct behavior in the normal case. However, if the linker mis-applied (or skipped) RVC relaxation due to the coarse-grained EF_RISCV_RVC flag — exactly the vulnerability in the spec — the kernel module loader will blindly load and execute the corrupted instruction stream with no detection, no checksum, and no fallback. The kernel implicitly trusts linker output with no defense-in-depth.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/module.c:575-575  (none — reloc_handlers table)
      Wires apply_r_riscv_relax_rela into the static reloc_handlers dispatch table at index R_RISCV_RELAX. Infrastructure is correct — the dispatch table entry maps the right relocation type to the right handler. The risk is in the handler itself (see above), not in the wiring.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/machine_kexec_file.c:239-243  arch_kexec_apply_relocations_add
      R_RISCV_RELAX is silently skipped (break) alongside R_RISCV_ALIGN and R_RISCV_PCREL_LO12_I, under the assumption the linker has already resolved it. Same trust-blinker-output pattern as the module loader: if the linker corrupted a norvc/rvc relaxation due to the EF_RISCV_RVC coarse-grained flag, kexec will load the corrupted purgatory code and jump into it. The kexec purgatory is particularly sensitive because it runs at a privilege boundary during kernel handover.
  [OK] drivers/firmware/efi/libstub/Makefile:148-148  (none — Makefile)
      Includes R_RISCV_RELAX in the STUBCOPY_RELOC filter so the EFI stub copy logic correctly handles this relocation type. This is a build-system filter, not runtime logic. It ensures the stub copy step doesn't choke on R_RISCV_RELAX entries. The filter itself is correct — the underlying risk (trusting linker output) is upstream of this Makefile.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:14-14  (none — header)
      Defines RISCV_ISA_EXT_c = ('c' - 'a') = 2, the numeric ID for the C extension. This is a simple constant used throughout feature-detection infrastructure. Not related to the linker relaxation path — the kernel uses this to detect hardware C support, not to decide per-instruction transformations. Correct and harmless.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:447-451  (none — riscv_c_exts array)
      Declares riscv_c_exts[] = {ZCA, ZCF, ZCD} as sub-extensions bundled under C, wired at line 499 via __RISCV_ISA_EXT_SUPERSET. This is hardware ISA feature validation: when the kernel detects C on a hart, it automatically enables Zca/Zcf/Zcd. This is correct per the Zc* spec (C implies Zca; C+F implies Zcf on RV32; C+D implies Zcd). This code is about CPU capability detection, not about linker relaxation decisions — it does not contribute to the described vulnerability.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/sys_hwprobe.c:91-92  hwprobe_isa_ext0
      Queries riscv_isa_extension_available(NULL, c) to populate RISCV_HWPROBE_IMA_C in the userspace hwprobe ABI. This correctly reports per-CPU C-extension hardware availability. While userspace could independently make the same coarse-grained-vs-fine-grained mistake as the linker (using this flag to decide per-instruction transformations), that is a userspace bug, not a kernel bug. The kernel correctly reports hardware truth.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:31-31  (none — kvm_isa_ext_arr)
      Maps KVM_RISCV_ISA_EXT_C → RISCV_ISA_EXT_c in the KVM-to-guest ISA extension translation table. This exposes the C extension to KVM guests as an available ISA feature. Correct infrastructure — the mapping is accurate. The potential risk (guest using coarse-grained C availability to decide per-instruction behavior) is a guest problem, not a host kernel problem.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:177-183  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_disable_allowed
      C extension listed in the 'cannot disable' fall-through case in kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_disable_allowed(), with the explicit comment 'Extensions which don't have any mechanism to disable'. This is architecturally correct — there is no CSR bit to disable the C extension per-vCPU, unlike features gated by senvcfg/henvcfg. Guest-visible ISA consistency is maintained. Not related to the linker relaxation vulnerability.

